    "result_text": "The full suite is green (3 tests, 0 failures, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization on owner creation, keeping each piece of logic in a small single-responsibility unit and reusing it across the pipeline and household identity.\n\n**New code**\n\n- `OwnerAddress` (`rest/function/owner/OwnerAddress.java`) \u2014 the reusable normalization utility, mirroring the existing `OwnerEmail`/`OwnerTelephone` helpers. `normalize()` trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-token abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). It's token-based, so `STREET` isn't re-matched against `ST`. A null/blank-after-trim address yields `\"\"`.\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress` (`rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerAddress.java`) \u2014 a pipeline step that applies `OwnerAddress.normalize` in place on the already-published body, mirroring `NormalizeOwnerEmail`.\n\n**Wiring**\n\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted `normalizeAddress` between `normalizeEmail` and `validate`. Running before validation means:\n  - the stored/returned `address` is the normalized form;\n  - an address blank after normalization (e.g. `\"   \"` \u2192 `\"\"`) is rejected by the existing required-field check in `ValidateOwnerFields` (400);\n  - every downstream address comparison sees the normalized value.\n\n**Household identity**\n\n- `Household.key(...)` now canonicalizes the address via `OwnerAddress.normalize`, so duplicate-household detection (`EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold`) and the shared household id both use the normalized form. The last-name canonicalization is unchanged.\n\n**How the tests map:** `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`, `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`, and `\"   \"` \u2192 400.\n\nOne behavioral note worth flagging: because normalization upper-cases, all stored addresses (including the default `\"N Test Street\"` from the test helper) are now persisted and returned upper-cased \u2014 that's the intended spec change, and no existing test asserted the old lower-case form.",
